---
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide class="center" style="--scale-from: 3.5;--fragment-duration: 1s">
  <h3
    p-effect="zoom fade"
    style="font-size: 0.6em; opacity: 0.6; margin-block-end: 0.5em;"
  >
    your MCP server is on the internet now...
  </h3>
  <h2 p-effect="zoom fade" style="font-size: 1.5em;">
    <span class="main-gradient">who's allowed to call it?</span> 🤔
  </h2>
  <p
    p-effect="zoom fade"
    style="font-size: 0.6em; margin-block-start: 1em; opacity: 0.7;"
  >
    MCP answers this with <strong>OAuth 2.1</strong>
  </p>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      stdio is inherently more controlled: the host starts a local process and
      communicates with it directly. But a remote HTTP MCP server is exposed
      over the network, so we need a standard way to authorize clients and
      users.
    </p>
    <p>
      MCP does not invent a custom auth mechanism here. It builds on OAuth 2.1
      and standard OAuth metadata discovery.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide class="center">
  <h2 style="margin-block-end: 0.2rem;" class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
    HTTP Authentication
  </h2>
  <h3 style="margin-block-start: 0.8rem;">OAuth 2.1 🔐</h3>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      For remote MCP servers, authorization is handled at the HTTP transport
      layer. The MCP server behaves like a protected resource server. The OAuth
      authorization server can be the same application, or an external identity
      provider.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">The OAuth flow in MCP</h3>
  <ul style="font-size: 0.8em">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🚪 <span class="main-sub-gradient">1. Unauthorized request</span> — client
      calls the MCP endpoint without a token
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🔍 <span class="main-sub-gradient">2. Resource discovery</span> — server
      points to its protected resource metadata
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧭 <span class="main-sub-gradient">3. Auth server discovery</span> —
      client finds authorization and token endpoints
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🪪 <span class="main-sub-gradient">4. Client registration</span> — client
      ID as a metadata URL (CIMD), DCR as fallback
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      👤 <span class="main-sub-gradient">5. Authorization</span> — user logs in
      and grants permission
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🎫 <span class="main-sub-gradient">6. Token exchange</span> — client gets
      an access token using PKCE
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      ✅ <span class="main-sub-gradient">7. Authenticated requests</span> —
      token sent as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      The important MCP-specific part is the discovery chain. The client does
      not start by guessing the authorization endpoint. First it discovers the
      protected resource metadata for the MCP server.
    </p>
    <p>
      That metadata tells the client which authorization server should be used.
      Then the client reads the authorization server metadata to find the login
      endpoint, token endpoint, supported PKCE methods, and so on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Client registration changed in the latest specs. The preferred way is now
      Client ID Metadata Documents: the client_id is an HTTPS URL pointing to a
      JSON document describing the client, so there's no registration call at
      all. Dynamic Client Registration is deprecated in 2026-07-28 and kept only
      as a fallback for authorization servers that don't support CIMD.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two hardening details: the client sends the resource parameter (RFC 8707)
      so the token is bound to this MCP server, and it validates the iss
      parameter in the authorization response (RFC 9207).
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      1. Protected Resource Metadata
    </span>
    🔍
  </h3>

  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp"
  >
  <code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-json">{
      "resource": "https://my-mcp-server.com/mcp",
      "authorization_servers": [
        "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com"
      ],
      "scopes_supported": [
        "orders:read",
        "orders:write"
      ],
      "bearer_methods_supported": [
        "header"
      ]
    }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is the MCP-specific discovery step. The MCP server is the protected
      resource. It exposes metadata that tells the client which authorization
      server can issue valid tokens for this resource.
    </p>
    <p>
      The key field here is authorization_servers. It points to the OAuth
      authorization server. This can be the same origin as the MCP server, or a
      separate identity provider.
    </p>
    <p>
      Note the path: for a server at /mcp the document lives at
      /.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp (RFC 9728 path insertion).
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      2. Authorization Server Metadata
    </span>
    🧭
  </h3>

  <pre
    class="small-line"
    data-label-file="https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/.well-known/oauth-authorization-server"
  >
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-json">
  {
    "issuer": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com",
    "authorization_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/authorize",
    "token_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/token",
    "registration_endpoint": "https://auth.my-mcp-server.com/oauth/register",
    "response_types_supported": ["code"],
    "code_challenge_methods_supported": ["S256"]
  }</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is standard OAuth authorization server metadata. Once the MCP client
      knows which authorization server to use, it fetches this document to learn
      where to redirect the user, where to exchange the authorization code, and
      whether dynamic client registration is supported.
    </p>
    <p>
      S256 is the PKCE method we care about. The client creates a code verifier,
      sends the code challenge during authorization, and later proves possession
      of the verifier during the token exchange.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 0.6em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      Becoming an OAuth provider
    </span>
    🛡️
  </h3>

  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="server.js">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">  import { betterAuth } from "better-auth";
  import { jwt } from "better-auth/plugins";
  import { oauthProvider } from "@better-auth/oauth-provider";
  import { cimd } from "@better-auth/cimd";

  const auth = betterAuth({
    baseURL: AUTH_BASE_URL,
    secret: process.env.BETTER_AUTH_SECRET,
    disabledPaths: ["/token"],
    plugins: [
      </code><p-fragment><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">jwt({ disableSettingJwtHeader: true }),
      oauthProvider({
        loginPage: "/sign-in",
        consentPage: "/consent",
        allowDynamicClientRegistration: true,
        allowUnauthenticatedClientRegistration: true,
      }),
      cimd(),
    ],
    socialProviders: {
      github: {
        clientId: process.env.GITHUB_ID,
        clientSecret: process.env.GITHUB_SECRET,
      },
    },
  });</code></p-fragment></pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      In this example, better-auth becomes the authorization server. It handles
      login, consent, token issuance, and dynamic client registration.
    </p>
    <p>
      The MCP server itself is still the protected resource. It does not need to
      implement the whole OAuth flow manually. It only needs to advertise where
      authorization happens and validate the access tokens it receives.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cimd plugin lets clients use a Client ID Metadata Document URL as
      their client_id — the method preferred by the current spec.
    </p>
    <p>
      Dynamic client registration (with unauthenticated public clients) stays
      enabled only for backwards compatibility with older MCP clients: DCR is
      deprecated as of 2026-07-28.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Verifying the token</span>
    🎫
  </h3>

  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="server.js">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">  import { verifyBearerToken } from "better-auth/oauth2";

  const verifier = {
    async verifyAccessToken(token) {
      const jwt = await verifyBearerToken(token, {
        jwksUrl: `${AUTH_BASE_URL}/jwks`,
        verifyOptions: {
          issuer: AUTH_BASE_URL,
          audience: MCP_RESOURCE_URL,
        },
      });

      return {
        token,
        clientId: jwt.azp,
        scopes: jwt.scope?.split(" ") ?? [],
        expiresAt: jwt.exp,
      };
    },
  };</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      The MCP SDK doesn't care how tokens are validated: it asks for a verifier
      with a single method, verifyAccessToken, that returns who the caller is.
    </p>
    <p>
      Here better-auth checks signature, issuer, audience and expiry against the
      authorization server's JWKS. The audience must be this MCP server: a token
      issued for another resource is rejected.
    </p>
    <p>Don't forget expiresAt: the SDK refuses tokens without it.</p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide follow-fragments>
  <h3 style="margin-block-end: 1em;">
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">Protecting the endpoint</span>
    🔒
  </h3>

  <pre class="small-line" data-label-file="server.js">
<code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">  import {
    requireBearerAuth,
    mcpAuthMetadataRouter,
    getOAuthProtectedResourceMetadataUrl,
  } from "@modelcontextprotocol/express";

  const resourceUrl = new URL(MCP_RESOURCE_URL);

  app.all("/api/auth/*", toNodeHandler(auth));
  </code><code p-fragment p-effect="highlight" style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">app.use(mcpAuthMetadataRouter({ oauthMetadata, resourceServerUrl: resourceUrl }));</code><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">

  app.all(
    "/mcp",
    </code><code p-fragment p-effect="highlight" style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">requireBearerAuth({
      verifier,
      resourceMetadataUrl: getOAuthProtectedResourceMetadataUrl(resourceUrl),
    }),</code><code style="font-size: 0.6em" is:raw class="language-javascript">
    (req, res) => node(req, res, req.body)
  );</code>
  </pre>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      SDK v2 ships the resource-server plumbing. mcpAuthMetadataRouter publishes
      the protected resource metadata document we saw earlier.
    </p>
    <p>
      requireBearerAuth sits in front of the MCP handler: it reads the Bearer
      token, runs our verifier, and on failure answers 401 with a
      WWW-Authenticate header pointing to resource_metadata — or 403
      insufficient_scope if required scopes are missing.
    </p>
    <p>
      From there, the client discovers the authorization server, performs the
      OAuth flow with PKCE, obtains an access token, and retries the MCP
      request. Inside tool handlers the verified identity is available as
      ctx.http.authInfo.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span class="cover-internal-sub-section-all">
      Don't build OAuth from scratch
    </span>
    🙏
  </h3>

  <ul>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📦 <span class="main-sub-gradient">Pick an auth layer</span>
      <span class="item-sub">
        <code>@auth/core</code> / <code>better-auth</code>
      </span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      ☁️ <span class="main-sub-gradient">Or use an identity provider</span>
      <span class="item-sub">
        <code>Clerk</code> / <code>Auth0</code> / <code>Keycloak</code>
      </span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      🧰 MCP SDK v2 ships{" "}
      <span class="main-sub-gradient">bearer auth &amp; metadata helpers</span>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Implementing OAuth from scratch is easy to get wrong. Use a library or a
      hosted provider whenever possible.
    </p>
    <p>
      The MCP-specific part is not “invent OAuth again”. It is wiring standard
      OAuth discovery, protected resource metadata, token validation, and the
      MCP transport together.
    </p>
    <p>
      The TypeScript SDK v2 covers the resource-server side (bearer middleware,
      protected resource metadata). The authorization server — login, consent,
      token issuance — is still a job for an auth library or an identity
      provider.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<style lang="scss">
  ul {
    li[p-fragment]:not(:last-of-type) {
      margin-block-end: 0.75em;
    }
  }

  .item-sub {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.72em;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--ink-mute, #484848);
    margin-inline-start: 1.5em;
    margin-block-start: 0.2em;

    code {
      font-style: normal;
      font-size: 0.95em;
    }
  }
</style>
